comments.body:
This entire conversation misses the point. I know that the panelists are more knowledgeable than they appear here.

THE central issue with online participation is DATA MANAGEMENT. Digital media companies collect user data then sell/trade it in the data marketplace. They get very wealthy; users get nothing (because the courts have often ruled that data from individuals is worthless).

Companies & the government should be held responsible to 5 key data management practices:
1) No secret collection. That means FULL DISCLOSURE to and WILLING & KNOWLEDGABLE AGREEMENT by users.” Neither “if you click here, you give us permission” nor pages on pages of privacy policies only the lawyers who wrote them can understand suffice;
2) The data may be used ONLY FOR THE USE IT WAS GIVEN. I disclose stuff to an online seller for the purpose of acquiring a product or service. I don’t thereby give permission to them to sell/trade my data;
3) Users have a right to know (a) what data was collected (b) what was done with it (c) who it was shared with;
4) Users have the right to correct inaccurate data;
5) Companies that collect user data are responsible to protect it from loss, leakage, and misuse.

The government proposed these “rules” at the start of the digital era, then ignored them (except in medical and educational settings). Why? That’s a long, political/intelligence story worth learning about. 
WE THE PEOPLE (a) got sold down the river & (b) refused to heed warnings of the outcomes.
